#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1944db5d-5dbb-5268-81cc-0ce9a0503edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4.5y=3{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333219} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea66d73-08a4-526b-8c6d-aaa97b9b7f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4.5y=12{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333710} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa2a08d-f61a-5f63-8878-f2e9f1fa96ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4.5y=6{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334358} {:answer/id #uuid "d02a9da6-04d1-5120-b8a8-87bf90b8110c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y=3{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334805} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0e4ecf-325b-59bd-90a8-d08faeba5d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y=12{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1944db5d-5dbb-5268-81cc-0ce9a0503edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4.5y=3{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333219} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1b9c5a-ea8e-581e-b135-39bd76d3bc2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07b06f33-b4ee-5f40-b29c-4546e0a946ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-64599" :question/problem "A particle of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=12{t}^{3}\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n4{y}^{″}=-18y+12{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4.5y=3{t}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a28c4186-c812-5117-83c3-943c9871c988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334419} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4057bb-935e-5406-ae84-0e99a9b2feff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334798} {:answer/id #uuid "d13828c4-d92d-5786-a8a2-c5df12308498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334819} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d2a3b1-1549-5cd5-ad78-61414e7f333c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334880} {:answer/id #uuid "f1aff7fe-125a-5328-8e93-9bb5ada34908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1aff7fe-125a-5328-8e93-9bb5ada34908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335109} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cbe8b04-b9bb-5077-ad66-1fa7004e8e05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23f2ae10-1325-5e77-a3d0-434c6abe321a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64600" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=12\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=9\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-9{y}^{′}-12y+15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3922d0-9e98-50fc-badf-c5493ce5c08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354795} {:answer/id #uuid "66a11be1-b8f6-5bf3-865a-32797a40b01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355576} {:answer/id #uuid "81aec1db-2243-5668-a3b3-f7f2049fd7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355803} {:answer/id #uuid "9e22c7d3-b846-5397-9fb1-05bb1733229d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356074} {:answer/id #uuid "b95128ac-6579-590f-8e02-4fa489a727c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b95128ac-6579-590f-8e02-4fa489a727c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356287} :answer-field/id #uuid "03b4326e-a9f7-5be8-a7ee-fabfc0ce13c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbe86c7f-4360-5aea-8ad0-c457c70df4e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94382" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(25t+ϕ)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$ It can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}]}
